# Carrier Wound #37: The Verifier Was Blinded to the One Artifact It Exists to Redact-Check. Two Modules Disagreed on Which File Is the Evidence.

Wound #36 pulled the phantom horizon check out of the platform, and the next
live drive sailed *through* the generated-workflow proof — past every check that
had blocked the prior dozen runs — and landed on the verifier with a clean plan
and a clean execution proof. And there the verifier did exactly what it is built
to do: it refused to bless what it could not see.

The deployed dream run went terminal:

```
run-live-20260614T235214125Z-25dd3778 → blocked: capability_denied
  "Output Evidence Snapshots do not include the report/hitl-report.mdsvx
   artifact content, so the verifier cannot confirm the public Wzrrd report is
   redacted, noindex via the declared template, cites receipt metadata, and
   follows docs/dream-report-canon.md proof-below-dreams expectations."
```

This block is **correct**. The verifier's job at this gate is to certify that
the public-facing Wzrrd report — the one artifact that *leaves the system and
gets indexed by the open web* — carries its noindex template, redacts every
private name and credential, and keeps proof below dreams per
`docs/dream-report-canon.md`. The report is `report/hitl-report.mdsvx`. The
verifier was handed Output Evidence Snapshots that **did not contain it.** A
verifier asked to certify redaction on a document it cannot read must block.
This is the inverse of a hollow-capture wound: the verifier was right, and the
carrier had starved it. (Removing the check to make the run go green would have
published an unredacted, web-indexable report — the exact catastrophe the check
exists to prevent. Forbidden.)

## The wound: two modules disagreed on which artifact is the evidence

The "which artifact content reaches the verifier, and how much of it" decision
lived in **two** modules that had silently drifted apart:

- **The loader.** `loadArtifactRefVerifierEvidence`
  (`src/app/application/workflow-app.ts`) decided *which* artifact refs become
  Output Evidence Snapshots. It carried a skip clause —
  `artifactRef.endsWith(".mdsvx")` — that dropped the report on the floor before
  it ever became evidence. So the safety-critical document never entered the
  snapshot set.
- **The prompt builder.** `verifierPromptFor`
  (`src/app/infrastructure/cloudflare-agent-lane-adapters.ts`) decided *how much*
  of each evidence text the verifier sees. It head-truncated **every** snapshot
  at a flat 2500 chars. The report's redaction and proof markers — the noindex
  template tag, the "## What did not happen" redaction section, the proof block —
  live in the report's **tail.** So even if the loader had passed the mdsvx
  through, the prompt builder would have sliced off exactly the part the verifier
  needed and re-blocked on a hidden tail. Two independent blinds, stacked.

The class is **information leakage — the file-format variant**, Ousterhout's most
pernicious kind, the back-door leak that lives in no interface:

> "Suppose two classes both have knowledge of a particular file format (perhaps
> one class reads files in that format and the other class writes them). Even if
> neither class exposes that information in its interface, they both depend on the
> file format: if the format changes, both classes will need to be modified.
> Back-door leakage like this is more pernicious than leakage through an
> interface, because it isn't obvious. Information leakage is one of the most
> important red flags in software design… ask yourself 'How can I reorganize
> these classes so that this particular piece of knowledge only affects a single
> class?'"
> — John Ousterhout, *A Philosophy of Software Design*, §Information Leakage

The "particular file format" here is *the hitl-report* — what it is, where its
safety-critical content sits, and therefore how it must be presented as evidence.
The loader and the prompt builder both held an opinion about that document, and
neither opinion appeared in either interface. The loader's opinion was "the mdsvx
isn't evidence" (wrong). The prompt builder's opinion was "all evidence is
front-loaded, head-truncation is lossless enough" (wrong for the one document
where the safety signal lives in the tail). When two modules silently encode the
same file's handling and disagree, the system is broken in exactly the way the
disagreement allows — here, a verifier blind to the document it must certify.

## How the leak got planted: a matched pair with a wrong half

The `.mdsvx` skip was not ancient. It was introduced two commits back in
`d8dc890` as a **matched pair** with `primaryOutputPathForStep`, which redirects
the hitl-report node's "primary" output to its `.json` sidecar. The pairing
carried an assumption: *"the json sidecar is sufficient evidence; the mdsvx is
just a rendering, skip it."*

Half of that pair is right and stays. `primaryOutputPathForStep` (json sidecar =
primary) is **correct** for presence-and-hash matching — the json is the
structured, stable artifact for "did this node produce its output." It is
**untouched.**

The other half is wrong. noindex-via-template, redaction completeness,
proof-below-dreams, receipt-citation — these are properties of the **rendered
mdsvx**, not the json. The json sidecar cannot answer "is the *public report*
redacted," because the public report is the mdsvx. Skipping the mdsvx didn't drop
a redundant rendering; it dropped the only artifact that carries the safety
contract the verifier enforces. The assumption "json is sufficient evidence"
conflated *presence evidence* (json: sufficient) with *redaction evidence* (mdsvx:
mandatory).

## The fix: additive, two-part, and it does NOT weaken the verifier

The fix restores the verifier's sight without removing a single check, and
without touching `primaryOutputPathForStep`.

**Part 1 — un-skip the mdsvx in the loader.** The `.endsWith(".mdsvx")` clause is
removed from `loadArtifactRefVerifierEvidence`'s skip condition. The report now
becomes an Output Evidence Snapshot. The `readJson` attempt fails on mdsvx and
falls through to `readText`, which tags it `mediaType: "text/mdsvx"` and stamps
`hash: sha256Hex(text)`. The json sidecar still flows through as before — this is
purely additive, both artifacts now reach the verifier.

**Part 2 — media-type-aware full presentation in the prompt builder.** A flat
2500-char head-truncation cannot serve a redaction target: the verifier must
confirm *nothing un-redacted lives anywhere* in the report, and a head-only view
could falsely bless a document whose raw private content hides in the truncated
tail — which then gets published. So `verifierPromptFor` now branches on media
type:

- `text/mdsvx` → presented in full up to a 60k ceiling. The report is **bounded
  by construction** (capped findings/proposals, a bounded D2 figure), so it fits
  comfortably under the ceiling in practice.
- On the pathological overflow case, it emits a **legible block-marker** —
  `[REPORT TRUNCATED — exceeds verifier inline budget; redaction and noindex
  completeness cannot be confirmed for the omitted tail, so this report must
  block rather than be accepted]` — never a silent `[truncated]`. A report that
  blows the ceiling *blocks*; it is never falsely blessed on an unseen tail.
- Everything else → the prior 2500-char head-truncation, unchanged. The cost
  discipline that keeps non-safety evidence cheap is preserved.

### Why full presentation is safe downstream

The deterministic verifier was checked, not assumed. `evidenceHashMatches`
(`src/app/infrastructure/deterministic-verifier.ts`) special-cases only
`application/json`; `text/mdsvx` falls to the `sha256Hex(input.text) ===
input.expectedHash` branch. The added mdsvx evidence carries
`hash: sha256Hex(text)` and the deterministic verifier receives the **untruncated**
evidence, so the hash check passes cleanly. No hash mismatch is introduced by
presenting more text to the agent verifier; the two verifiers see consistent
content.

## The chaos tests that would have caught it (hostile transport, not polite mocks)

Two layers, each driving **real** production transport, each falsified (red on
its own bug, green on the fix):

- **Layer 1 — the loader, end-to-end.** In `tests/integration/workflow-app.test.ts`,
  the real end-to-end dream run (`executes generated Dream preflight steps through
  the configured memory fabric adapter`) now runs through the **real**
  `loadVerifierOutputEvidence`. It asserts the `report/hitl-report.mdsvx` snapshot
  exists, carries `mediaType "text/mdsvx"`, equals the full rendered report, and
  contains tail-resident markers (`## What did not happen`, `## Dynamic generation
  proof`) in the correct below-dreams order. **Falsified:** re-adding the
  `.mdsvx` skip turns it red — `expected undefined to be 'text/mdsvx'`. The fake
  verifier in this test blesses unconditionally (politer than pi), which is
  *exactly why* the old exclusion shipped green and undetected — a textbook
  `[[polite-fakes-franchise-wound]]`: the double never demanded the evidence pi
  demanded, so the missing artifact was invisible until a real verifier on a real
  drive refused it.
- **Layer 2 — the prompt builder, real assembly.** In
  `tests/integration/cloudflare-agent-lane-adapters.test.ts`, a new case drives
  the **real** `createCloudflarePiVerifierLaneAdapter(...).verify(...)` with three
  hostile evidence shapes: an in-budget mdsvx (~5k) with redaction/proof
  sentinels deliberately placed *past* 2500; a long plain-text snapshot (~3.2k)
  with a tail sentinel; and an overflow mdsvx (>60k). It captures the assembled
  prompt and asserts the mdsvx tail/redaction/proof markers **survive**, the plain
  text **is** head-truncated (`[truncated]`), and the overflow mdsvx emits the
  block-marker with its tail **excluded**. **Falsified:** reverting to flat-2500
  turns it red — `mdsvxTailSurvives: false`, `overflowBlockMarker: false`.

`pnpm typecheck` clean · `pnpm check` clean · full suite **440 passed / 55 files**.

## What this does NOT claim (skepticism held)

This restores the verifier's sight on the report and proves, in-suite, that the
report content reaches the verifier in full with its tail intact, that other
evidence stays cheaply truncated, and that an overflow report blocks loudly
rather than being blessed on an unseen tail. It does **not**, by itself, prove the
dream run reaches `captured` — only a live re-drive on the deployed fix does. The
hypothesis is concrete and falsifiable: the run blocked because the verifier could
not read the report it must redact-check; with the report now an untruncated
Output Evidence Snapshot, the verifier can confirm noindex/redaction/proof-order
and the run proceeds to `captured`. If instead the verifier *reads* the report and
finds a genuine redaction or noindex defect, that is a new, true signal about the
report generator — not this carrier wound, and not to be silenced by re-blinding
the verifier. One fresh deploy carries the change; the next live drive says which.

## The carrier discipline (seventeenth carrier wound)

#35 fixed *whether the verifier could speak at all.* #36 fixed *a check that
refused every answer once it could.* #37 fixes *what the verifier is allowed to
see before it answers* — and the class is not "the mdsvx was skipped." It is
"**two modules silently encoded how the safety-critical report is handled, and
they disagreed, so the verifier was starved of the one artifact it exists to
certify.**" The fix is Ousterhout's information-hiding as a carrier invariant
again, one layer down from #36: when a single document's safety properties drive
both *which artifact is evidence* and *how much of it the verifier sees*, that
knowledge cannot be split across a loader that drops it and a truncator that
guts it. The safety-critical module gets the artifact, in full, or it blocks
loudly — never a silent head-slice that could bless an unredacted tail into the
public web. A verifier you have blinded is worse than no verifier: it doesn't
refuse, it *approves* — and approval is the one outcome that ships the harm.
